# RTL8852BS2 WPA2-PSK: carry the association of the parent profile into the
# four-way handshake of IEEE 802.11 clause 12.7.6, require the access point's
# third EAPOL-Key message to carry an integrity code this host verifies, and
# install the two keys that handshake produced into the hardware.
#
# The parent is the association profile whose whole chain already passed on the
# board, so one run of this image re-validates everything proven before -
# firmware download, the runtime control plane, the no-link role and CAM, the
# passive 2.4 GHz 1-13 sweep with real Beacon RX, the wlan0 netdev whose wapi
# scan reports those access points, the Probe Request/Response exchange, the
# directed Authentication Request/Response, the JOININFO + ADDR_CAM_UPDATE, and
# the Association Request that came back with an association identifier - and
# then adds the four frames of the handshake.
#
# The handshake replaces the association step rather than following it. An
# access point sends its first EAPOL-Key frame within milliseconds of granting
# the association, so the only place it can be answered is the receive loop of
# the sweep the association happened in; a separate step would spend a whole
# extra sweep to arrive too late for the frame it exists to answer.
#
# What this host computes: the pairwise master key as PBKDF2-SHA1 over the
# passphrase salted with the SSID for 4096 iterations, once before any sweep
# because it costs some sixteen thousand SHA-1 compressions; the pairwise
# transient key as PRF-384 over that key with the two addresses and the two
# nonces each in sorted order; and the integrity code of every EAPOL-Key frame
# as HMAC-SHA1-128 under the key confirmation key with the code's own field read
# as zero. All of it is this port's own code - SHA-1, HMAC, PBKDF2 and the PRF
# included - and all of it was verified on the host against the published
# IEEE 802.11i test vectors and an independent implementation before this
# profile existed.
#
# What proves it: the integrity code on the access point's third message. The
# access point can only compute that from a pairwise key derived from the same
# pairwise master key, over the same bytes, in the same order, so one
# verification confirms the passphrase, the derivation, the key expansion, the
# nonce and address ordering, and every byte of the second message this host
# transmitted, all at once. The second message's RSN element is the very bytes
# of the Association Request's, as clause 12.7.2 requires.
#
# The four EAPOL-Key frames are themselves unprotected, so the handshake needs
# no security engine in order to happen. The keys it produces do, and this
# image brings that engine up before anything is transmitted: the three cipher
# clocks, the six direction enables, integrity-code and message-integrity
# append, and partial transmit mode off. The group key arrives wrapped inside
# the third message and is opened with the AES key unwrap of RFC 3394 under the
# key encryption half of the transient key - only after the integrity code has
# verified, which is what rules out a wrong passphrase before anything is
# decrypted at all. Both keys then go into the hardware the way the vendor's
# mac_sta_add_key() does it: the address CAM entry is re-sent with the key's
# slot filled in, then the security CAM entry holding the key is written, for
# the pairwise key first and the group key second.
#
# The passphrase is not here. CONFIG_K1_RTL8852BS2_RUNTIME_WPA_PASSPHRASE and
# CONFIG_K1_RTL8852BS2_RUNTIME_WPA_SSID are left empty by this profile on
# purpose - a credential belongs in nobody's repository - and this image is
# built through tools/build_k1_wpa.sh, which reads K1_WIFI_PASSPHRASE and
# K1_WIFI_SSID from outside the source tree and appends them to a generated
# defconfig that lives outside it as well. Built as it stands, the step reports
# ENOKEY and runs no handshake, which is the honest result for an image with no
# key.
#
# Nothing is printed that would leak one either: not the passphrase, not the
# pairwise master key, and none of the transient key or its three sub-keys. The
# console carries which frame arrived, which one went out, and whether the
# integrity code verified.
#
# The association also programs CMAC port 0 now, which is the band 0 / port 0
# infrastructure subset of the vendor's mac_port_init() and the first time this
# port writes that register block rather than only reading it. It runs where
# upstream runs it - after JOININFO and the address CAM update, on the
# association that granted an identifier - and every field it programs has to
# read back. Most of the block already holds what a station wants, left there
# by the firmware's own bring-up, so what actually changes is the network type,
# the function enable, the two beacon report enables and the prohibit window's
# hold and setup times; the console names each field with what it held and what
# it holds afterwards. The two absences are named rather than dropped: the
# disable flow cannot apply to a port initialised once per boot, and
# mac_wde_pkt_drop()'s body is not in the vendor source this port was written
# against, so the high-queue release is a read of the beacon drop register and a
# logged no-op.
#
# The same run therefore also settles what the earlier profiles could only note:
# the port's TSF was never frozen. It reads as the access point's own timer well
# before this step, because the receive BSSID filter and the TSF update enable
# were already set and the address CAM holds the BSSID, and dly_port_us() - the
# TSF-based wait mac_port_init() places between the function enable and the
# beacon early time - is what says so from inside the sequence.
#
# After all of that, and for the first time in this port, the image stays on the
# channel with no sweep running. Every frame any earlier run received or
# transmitted happened inside a channel dwell, and the reason was assumed to be
# that only the firmware tunes the radio; the RF snapshots of the association
# runs say otherwise, because the channel register holds the parked channel
# between sweeps and each sweep starts on the value the previous one ended with.
# What ends with a sweep is this port's own receive filter, restored to a value
# that no longer accepts the broadcast frames a Beacon is one of. So the window
# widens that filter again, polls the receive FIFO for three seconds, transmits
# one directed Probe Request into it, and requires a Beacon from the associated
# access point and that access point's Probe Response back. It tunes nothing:
# the channel and bandwidth registers of all three layers are sampled on both
# sides and compared, the console names any that moved, and the only registers
# written are the three receive filters, all of which are put back. A silent
# window is reported as what it is - ENODATA for no Beacon, ETIMEDOUT for a
# Beacon with no Probe Response, EIO for a register that moved - with beacons
# from other access points, data frames from the target and any
# Deauthentication with its reason code counted apart, and it does not retract
# the association and handshake that were already reported.
#
# This is still not a working link. The keys are installed and acknowledged by
# the firmware, and the port is enabled, which is all this claims: nothing is
# encrypted or decrypted yet, because the transmit descriptor's security fields
# are not filled in and there is no data path to fill them for. No data frame
# beyond the unprotected EAPOL-Key frames is sent or received, no wlan0
# behaviour changes and no IP address is configured. Still RAM-only: nothing
# written to eMMC, SPI flash, eFuse or the U-Boot environment.

#include "../wireless_assoc_diag/defconfig"

CONFIG_K1_RTL8852BS2_RUNTIME_PORT_INIT_DIAGNOSTIC=y
CONFIG_K1_RTL8852BS2_RUNTIME_RESIDENT_DIAGNOSTIC=y
CONFIG_K1_RTL8852BS2_RUNTIME_WPA_DIAGNOSTIC=y
